{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"fc5e731f8484826b7a626af97b0a6b55f3c400778ead24397aea2b1ffcd2c0d4","slug":"index-10","location":"/chapters/ch10_visual_features/0_index.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"10 - Image Features","authors":[{"id":"Roger Weber","name":"Roger Weber"}],"content_includes_title":false,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"thumbnail":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/plot_10_10-334aac2a42b6bed3ff3eafd2f998ff0c.jpg","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"0_index.md","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/0_index-81ff73d1a1af33dfce82115f9948fe2f.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Take the photo of the Matterhorn in ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"CUvZKKX0yQ"},{"type":"crossReference","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"QGmHlUcaf9"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"ogLwznXK99"}],"identifier":"fig-matterhorn-query","label":"fig-matterhorn-query","kind":"figure","template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"1","resolved":true,"html_id":"fig-matterhorn-query","key":"hStt0xX27U"},{"type":"text","value":", drop it into a photo archive, and ask for “more like this”. The file holds 1280 by 853 pixels with three color values each, about 3.3 million numbers. Take the same photo again a few steps to the left: the mountain is still there, but almost every one of these numbers changes. A retrieval system cannot compare raw pixels. It needs a feature vector that stays stable when the camera moves or the light changes, and whose distances agree with what a user calls similar. Such features exist at two levels: perceptual features describe how an image looks, and learned embeddings capture what it shows. This chapter shows how both are computed, with the Matterhorn photo as the query throughout.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"ELz9fXjdcE"}],"key":"XzyN7daXfZ"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/plot_10_10-334aac2a42b6bed3ff3eafd2f998ff0c.jpg","alt":"The Matterhorn above Zermatt, the query photo of this chapter (photo: Dennis G. Jarvis, CC BY-SA 2.0).","width":"70%","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"NsfxkYmIql","urlSource":"images/plot_10_10.jpg"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"fig-matterhorn-query","identifier":"fig-matterhorn-query","html_id":"fig-matterhorn-query","enumerator":"1","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"mVbaIcMqg4"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"PZSqBFJVY0"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"qeyvnHUxUz"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"q0D3JDrMwO"},{"type":"text","value":"The Matterhorn above Zermatt, the query photo of this chapter (photo: Dennis G. Jarvis, CC BY-SA 2.0).","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"SBkXy4g03U"}],"key":"mDPc9GgTz9"}],"key":"LaGB6okB8B"}],"label":"fig-matterhorn-query","identifier":"fig-matterhorn-query","enumerator":"1","html_id":"fig-matterhorn-query","key":"ReNyr58xgG"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The harder problem is that “more like this” has more than one meaning. A designer may want photos with the same pale blue, white, and dark green. A hiker may want the same mountain from a different viewpoint. A travel agency may want any snow-covered peak, and a user without an example image has only words: “a pyramid-shaped peak above a pine forest”. Each of these wishes calls for a different feature.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"zquz1B5Icd"}],"key":"Jsiqhzl2Z0"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Image features developed in three generations, and each generation answered more of these wishes. The first generation measures what human vision responds to: the distribution of colors, the edges and textures that describe how intensities are arranged, and local keypoints that recognize the same object in another photo. Every one of these features was designed by hand. The second generation learns its features. A convolutional network trained to classify images ends up with first-layer filters that resemble the hand-designed ones, and its later layers produce an image embedding that works for tasks the network was never trained on. The third generation, transformer encoders such as CLIP and DINO, is what image search runs on today. A final comparison sends one query through all three generations and shows what each one improved, and where the hand-designed features still win.","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"nvipDNPVp2"}],"key":"cKLpY0kG24"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"tip","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What you’ll learn","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"ewGfR1hhBV"}],"key":"Uh8jF4Mz3q"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain why the choice of color space decides whether color distances make sense, and compare images with color histograms and color moments.","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"uP4fQjp4Ez"}],"key":"ql3OUfLKsc"}],"key":"aecBWIX3OM"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe how gradients, Gabor filters, HOG, and SIFT capture the arrangement of intensities, and how SIFT finds the same object in another photo.","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"jloZvo49ZR"}],"key":"bNTwAy8ClN"}],"key":"K1oqj0cwGD"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how a pretrained convolutional network serves as a feature extractor, why its embeddings transfer to new tasks, and why they are not trained to compare images.","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"hxoMaHdlqR"}],"key":"eIIw9mlOgf"}],"key":"MIQUYQnQFn"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how CLIP and DINO learn image embeddings without class labels, and apply them to image-to-image search, text-to-image search, and near-duplicate detection.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"c73yE5Unon"}],"key":"rHmTOBZ4RE"}],"key":"ta2HS3zLOF"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evaluate which generation of image features fits a retrieval task, based on a comparison of all three on the same query.","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"cYqcol9Lzj"}],"key":"iH1PxmT2S1"}],"key":"YudEZeZM47"}],"key":"JueatiS70Z"}],"key":"fZsJUYY0QC"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"seealso","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"ty